docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:3:topic: twofield-water-splash-dynamics
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:7:# Two-field water: splash dynamics (make grid water read as water, not slime) — requirements
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:11:The two-field grid water **looks like slime, not water**: on impact it goops together into a coherent blob instead of throwing up a splash, and free water (in flight, and after it drips out of the V60 cone) moves as a damped mass rather than lively liquid. The water↔coffee/bed coupling already reads as plausible — **the defect is the water dynamics in isolation**. This is a **dissipation** problem in the velocity transfer, confirmed by the live-webapp visual oracle to persist on **both** the legacy default **and** the K=3 over-pack-fixed build. The *over-pack defect* and the *slime defect* are therefore separate problems — this work is **orthogonal to the bulk-density over-pack thread** (the uncapped two-sided density relief committed behind `dbg.w`, gated off), which is parked and **not** in scope here. (Note: orthogonality of the *defects* is not the same as energetic independence of the *code paths* — see R8.)
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:13:The fix stays **inside the existing two-field grid solver** (no hybrid, no particle-side surface, no sub-grid droplet method). The agreed target is **splash & rebound on impact**, which is grid-resolvable: it is an **energy-retention** problem, not a sub-grid-detail problem. The agreed appetite is **realism over minimal change** — we go for the principled fix (state-aware dissipation) and pull in the supporting levers as needed, rather than capping at the cheapest knob. The agreed settled-surface target is **gentle residual liveliness** — real water is never glass-still — which means we only have to kill the *pathological* swirl artifact, not all motion. That relaxation is what makes the splash↔settled-quiet trilemma tractable.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:21:- **APIC transfer with a 5% PIC blend** (`transfers.wgsl:191`): `apic_keep = 1 − pic_blend`, `PIC_BLEND_DEFAULT = 0.05` (`mod.rs:151`); `pic_blend = 1` is the pure-PIC (maximally dissipative) limit. The affine `C` field — APIC's energy carrier — is scaled by `0.95` every G2P, and the loss accumulates step over step, pulling the water toward a coherent goopy blob. There is **no FLIP component**: `g2p_water` gathers velocity from `grid_vel` and the only retained particle state is `C`, so the higher-frequency velocity modes a FLIP (Δv) update would preserve are filtered — and those higher modes *are* the splash.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:22:- **`max_speed` clamp applied at THREE sites per step** (`transfers.wgsl:93` P2G affine, `transfers.wgsl:203` G2P velocity, **`coupling.wgsl:292` `drag_fold`** post-gravity/drag/BC): caps the peak velocities that form a splash crown. Critically, the cap is **coupled to the fixed-point overflow headroom** (`common.wgsl:117`): every scattered momentum contribution is magnitude-clamped to `max_speed`, so the i32 node-lane overflow bound scales as ≈ `8192 / max_speed` mass-units. Raising the cap for splash directly *shrinks* the FP safety margin — its overflow-safety role must survive even as its splash-throttling role is relaxed.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:23:- **`nozzle_radius` jet widening — deliberately detuned against splash** (`web.rs:383`/`:412` CenterPour-twofield, `:420` WaterOnly-twofield): the web setup widens the jet to `0.55` (~3.4 cells) and lowers `max_speed` to `12` *on purpose*, because a sub-2-cell jet on a grid solver reconstructs noisy asymmetric velocities, whips/scatters/plunges, and traps an air column the pocket machinery then carries as a crushing bubble that collapses the pool. So the jet/clamp levers are **not free wins** — they sit where they are to suppress grid artifacts that masquerade as motion. Reversing them naively re-introduces noise, not water.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:26:**The trilemma (the real crux).** The 5% PIC blend was added *deliberately* to quiet a settled-pool **stirring artifact** (see `[[project_twofield_stirring_crater_coupling]]`). Energy retention that produces splash is the same energy retention that re-stirs the pool, so a single **global** knob cannot win both — dial it down for splash and the pool re-energizes. Breaking the trilemma is the central design problem.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:28:**A subtlety the fix must respect (two-source stir).** The settled-agitation characterization (`tests/twofield_settled.rs:11`) found the historical stir had **two** sources, not one: the affine `C` is the lossless energy **carrier** (pure PIC collapses the tail KE), *and* the density relief is a dominant energy **source** (turning relief off also collapses the tail KE, via the `cell_meta.x` pressure RHS in `pressure.wgsl:482` and the under-density suction in `surface.wgsl:175`). So "settled liveliness" energy can enter from the production density-relief path as well as from the transfer. The splash fix is built on the **transfer** path and must not *modify or depend on* the parked `dbg.w` path — but it cannot honestly claim zero liveliness comes from relief (see R8).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:32:- **Target = splash & rebound on impact; grid-resolvable; no hybrid.** Water striking a wall/bed/pool throws up a crown or sheet and scatters, instead of merging. This is an energy-retention problem solvable inside the grid solver. Sub-grid detail (droplets, spray, thin ligaments) is explicitly out of scope — a coarse collocated grid cannot resolve it, and reaching it would force a particle-side/hybrid method (the biggest scope), which we are **not** taking on.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:33:- **Energy retention becomes state-aware — this is the trilemma-breaker.** Make the transfer's high-frequency energy retention a function of local **dynamical state**: fast / airborne / impacting / near-surface water keeps its energy (toward pure APIC and/or a FLIP component); slow / deep / settled water stays damped (toward today's PIC blend). By construction, settled water gets more dissipation than splashing water, so splash returns *without* re-arming the settled pool. Conceptual prior art: **ASFLIP** (affine-augmented separable FLIP — a velocity-adaptive FLIP blend designed precisely to retain energy and enable separation/splash while keeping APIC stability) and **PolyPIC** (higher-moment, lower-dissipation transfer). Cited as the existence proof for the mechanism; exact references and the specific state signal are planning concerns.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:34:- **Settled target = gentle residual liveliness, not dead-still.** The protected invariant is **"no pathological swirl"** (a quantitative bound — see R11), **not** a glass-still surface. Soft ripples / micro-motion on a settled pool are *correct* and desirable. This relaxes the trilemma — the state-aware switch's settled arm need only be non-pathological, not fully damping — and lets the strict dead-still quietness gate be re-pointed to the agitation floor.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:35:- **Supporting levers are on the critical path but are NOT free.** Even a perfect transfer won't splash if the clamp caps the crown or the jet is too diffuse — so relaxing `max_speed` (all three sites) and sharpening the jet are in scope. But both were deliberately detuned to suppress grid artifacts: relaxing `max_speed` erodes fixed-point overflow headroom (≈ `8192 / max_speed`, `common.wgsl:117`), and sharpening the jet below a grid-resolvable diameter (~2 cells) re-introduces whip/scatter/plunge and air-pocket collapse. Each relaxation must therefore be gated by cap-hit-rate and FP-saturation proxies (not just "keep a backstop"), and the jet must keep a minimum grid-resolvable diameter — clarifying fixed-flow vs fixed-exit-speed behavior when narrowing — or pair with a resolution change.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:36:- **Explicit go/no-go on the grid-only bet.** The repo documents a grid-resolution floor around jet width (`web.rs:383`), so A+B+C are a *first bet*, not a guarantee. **Decision rule:** if transfer/clamp/nozzle changes fail to produce splash *without* hitting velocity-cap saturation or grid-noise artifacts, escalate to a resolution increase or a hybrid surface treatment rather than tuning indefinitely. The doc does **not** claim A+B+C are sufficient — only that they are the correct, lowest-blast-radius first attempt.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:37:- **Orthogonal to the over-pack/density thread (defect-level), separable at the code level.** This change lives in the transfer / clamp / jet, not in the pressure/coupling operator. It does not modify and does not depend on the parked `dbg.w` density-relief thread; isolation across relief modes is a gate (R8).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:38:- **Realism over minimal change (this scope only).** Per the user's explicit "whatever it takes for realism," we adopt the principled state-aware fix rather than stopping at the cheapest global knob — overriding the usual minimal-change default for this feature. The cheap levers (B, C below) are still pursued, but as bounded complements to the core fix, not as a substitute for it.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:44:- R2. The falling pour reads as a concentrated stream that impacts hard (craters/splashes), not a diffuse soft column that smears its momentum on landing — subject to the grid-resolvable jet floor (≳2-cell diameter).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:49:- R5. **State separation holds:** the energy retention that produces splash is gated to splashing water (fast / airborne / impacting / near-surface) and does not re-introduce pathological agitation in the settled pool. By construction, settled water is damped more than splashing water.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:53:- R7. Real-time budget holds (≤ 33 ms @ 200k) on the fixed-point i32 grid (no float atomics), under Tint uniformity (a state-dependent transfer branch must stay uniformity-safe). **Storage budget:** the hard WebGPU per-stage cap is **16** buffers; the twofield kernels run under a tighter *internal* **7-buffer** budget. A FLIP/ASFLIP arm likely needs the pre-update grid velocity — either decoded from the existing `grid_fp` (no new buffer, but extra reads/cost in `g2p_water`) or a new `grid_vel_prev` buffer (permissible only if it stays within the hard 16-cap and is explicitly budgeted). If neither fits, the core must be constrained to **pure-APIC state-gating** (blend → 0 where splashing), which needs no velocity history. The `max_speed` overflow-safety function (all three sites) is preserved even as its splash-throttling function is relaxed.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:54:- R8. **Independence from the parked density thread:** the splash fix neither modifies nor depends on the `dbg.w` over-pack/density-relief path. *Honest caveat:* with **production** relief on, some settled liveliness is *already* sourced by the relief/suction RHS (`pressure.wgsl:482`, `surface.wgsl:175`), so this requirement is **non-dependence and code-level separability**, NOT a claim that zero liveliness energy comes from relief. **Isolation gate:** splash behavior and the settled floor (R11) hold across the relief-on, relief-off, and `dbg.w`-on modes, and toggling `dbg.w` does not change the splash result.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:57:- R9. **Visual oracle (primary):** on the live webapp — pour into an empty cup → splash on wall/bottom impact; pour onto a settled pool → splash then settle to gentle liveliness; isolated falling water (post-cone) → lively. Per the team's workflow, the watery look is confirmed visually *before* invariants are pinned (no premature tests against an unsettled approach).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:58:- R10. A **non-gameable quantitative splash signal** distinguishes splash from goop. Raw velocity variance / upward component is insufficient (satisfiable by noisy non-water motion or velocity-cap artifacts), so the signal must be **mass-weighted and localized**: post-impact upward/outward free-surface water momentum (or rebound height / lateral spread of water mass) above a floor, conditioned on a **low velocity-cap-hit rate** (the motion is real, not saturation) AND passing the existing conservation / no-popcorn checks. Numeric thresholds are a planning calibration, pinned only after the visual approach settles.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:60:- R12. **Clamp-relaxation safety gates:** any `max_speed` raise is accompanied by a velocity-cap-hit-rate metric and a fixed-point saturation proxy (extend the FP-headroom probe at `tests/twofield_water.rs:335`), so a relaxation that buys splash by courting i32 overflow is caught as a failure, not shipped as a win.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:64:All grid-native; the XPBD/PBF particle solver is cited only as evidence that a particle method splashes more naturally (and as the reason the grid has a liveliness floor) — it is **not** ported or hybridized here.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:66:- **A — State-aware energy retention (core / recommended).** Make the transfer's high-frequency energy retention depend on local dynamical state, so splashing water keeps energy and settled water is damped. This is the principled trilemma-breaker and the realism bet. Conceptual basis: ASFLIP-style velocity-adaptive FLIP blend and/or PolyPIC higher moments, layered on the existing APIC transfer. *Open at planning:* the exact state signal (local speed? air/free-surface proximity? recent-impact flag?), the blend-curve shape, whether a true FLIP Δv component is needed or a state-gated pure-APIC arm (blend → 0 where splashing) suffices, and — if FLIP — the velocity-history storage strategy under the buffer budget (R7).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:67:- **B — Velocity-clamp relaxation (supporting, not free).** Raise `max_speed` on the splash path so the crown's peak velocities survive, at all three clamp sites (`transfers.wgsl:93`, `:203`, `coupling.wgsl:292`). Constrained by fixed-point headroom (`common.wgsl:117`: overflow bound ≈ `8192 / max_speed`) — so each raise is gated by cap-hit-rate and FP-saturation proxies (R12), not a blanket removal.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:70:**Recommendation:** A as the core, with B and C as necessary-but-bounded complements (a perfect transfer won't splash through a tight clamp or a diffuse jet, but the clamp and jet have hard floors). All three are a grid-side **first bet**, not a sufficiency guarantee. Apply the go/no-go (Key Decisions): if they fail *without* cap-saturation or grid-noise artifacts, escalate to resolution/hybrid rather than tuning indefinitely.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:75:- State-aware energy retention in the velocity transfer (the core splash fix).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:76:- `max_speed` clamp relaxation across all three sites (preserving overflow safety via R12) and jet sharpening down to the grid-resolvable floor.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:81:- Surface tension / cohesion (a droplet/sheet concern, not an impact-splash concern).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:83:- Grid refinement / resolution increase (perf risk against R7; the primary escalation target if A+B+C are insufficient for impact splash).
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:93:- **FLIP vs pure-APIC arm, and history storage:** does the splashing arm need a true FLIP Δv component (ASFLIP), or does state-gating to pure APIC (blend → 0) get there with less risk? If FLIP, is the pre-update grid velocity decodable from `grid_fp` (no new buffer) or does it need a budgeted `grid_vel_prev` within the hard 16-cap (R7)?
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:94:- **Two-source interaction:** the historical stir drew energy from both the affine carrier and the production density relief. Confirm the transfer-side switch delivers settled non-pathological liveliness across relief-on/off/`dbg.w` modes (R8) without re-coupling to the parked density path.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:97:- **Numeric thresholds** for R10 (splash signal), R11 (agitation floor), and R12 (cap-hit / FP-saturation) — calibration, pinned after the look settles.
docs/brainstorms/2026-06-18-twofield-water-splash-dynamics-requirements.md:98:- **Prior-art references:** confirm exact ASFLIP / PolyPIC citations and the most directly applicable formulation during planning research.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:22:**Symptom (observed in the V60 water-only scene under an active pour):** a thin layer of water creeps up the cup wall and persists above the bulk pool surface. Headless probe (radius-bin: wall water at `r≈3` vs bulk pool): baseline wall water reached `y ≈ −3.16` (above the cup rim at `−3.5`) while the pool sat ~3–4 units lower. The wall layer *accumulates* over time and recedes when the pour stops — i.e., a real climbing/sticking artifact, not a transient splash.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:43:- **R7.** No new instability: water incompressibility, long-run no-eruption, cone settling, V60 drainage, coupling, and wetting conservation all still hold.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:152:**Requirements:** R1, R2, R5, R7.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:202:**Requirements:** R2, R3, R4, R7.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:208:**Approach:** Add a no-climb gate reusing the existing water-only V60 scene and the radius-bin probe pattern from the diagnosis: run a pour, bin grain-free water by horizontal radius, and assert wall-region water stays near the bulk pool surface. **Use a sustained/quantile metric, not raw `max_y` (Codex r1):** a single splash particle can spike `max_y` under an active pour, so gate on the wall-region **95th/99th-percentile height** over a steady window, with a **minimum-occupancy threshold** so the test only fires on a persistent sheet (not transient spray). Baseline (no fix) climbed to `−3.16`; the heuristic reached `−3.63`; the structural fix should keep the wall-region quantile height at/below the cup rim (`−3.5`) and within a small band of the bulk pool surface. Also add a **sign diagnostic** (Codex r1) and a **floor-wall corner fill** test. Assert the existing boundary-density eruption prevention is intact. Re-run the full affected suites.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:211:- **No wall climb (replaces the heuristic):** V60 water-only pour, ~480 steps; over the steady window, the wall-region (`r∈[2.6,3.2]`) **95th/99th-percentile** water height stays ≤ the cup rim (`−3.5`) and within a small band of the bulk pool surface, gated by a minimum wall-region occupancy so transient splash does not trip or mask it. This is the gate that must pass with the heuristic removed.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:268:- **Codex review round 1** (gpt-5.5 high, read-only; `.deliberate/wall_review.md`) — **REVISE → folded**. Confirmed the core physics (derivative `ψ'(t)=−(315/256)(1−t²)⁴/h` correct; `sum_g` yes / `sum_g2` no correct; `compute_dp` `λ_i·g_b` only correct; Route B reasonable). Required revisions, all applied: (1) corrected the `g_b` sign wording (gradient points toward the wall; `λ_i·g_b` is the repulsion) in KTD-4/U3/Risks; (2) made the 16-buffer grant a hard failure in U2; (3) added a deterministic sign diagnostic to U6; (4) switched the no-climb gate to a 95th/99th-percentile + occupancy metric; (5) added a floor-wall corner fill test.
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:269:- **Codex review round 2** (gpt-5.5 high, read-only; `.deliberate/wall_review_r2.md`) — **REVISE → folded; physics confirmed acceptable**. Verified the round-1 revisions are substantively correct, that adding `g_b` to `sum_g` is the expected Jacobian for a fixed boundary (denominator change benign), and that per-iteration application is correct PBF (not a once-per-frame accumulation). Required plan-precision revisions, all applied: (1) fixed U2 sequencing — the `boundary_grad` bind-group entries move to U3/U4 where the shaders statically use them (auto-derived layouts reject an unused early binding); (2) strengthened the corner test to assert density-in-band / no-seam-sheet / no-leak-along-unselected-face with `d_side ≈ d_floor` sampling; (3) made the AABB guarantee an explicit byte-equality (set-comparison) test phrased as "no read / no arithmetic when `num_solids==0`."
docs/plans/2026-06-07-001-fix-pbf-wall-pressure-boundary-plan.md:270:- **Codex review round 3** (gpt-5.5 high, read-only; `.deliberate/wall_review_r3.md`) — **APPROVE**. Confirmed the round-2 revisions are incorporated correctly and the plan is implementation-ready (only a trivial R-ID typo, fixed).
