docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:49:- R7. The frame contains no converged global Poisson solve. Incompressibility cost is bounded: one coarse-grid solve (≤ ~1/64 of fine cell count) plus a fixed small number of fine local iterations.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:50:- R8. Dispatches/frame and max-storage-buffers-per-entry-point are first-class budgets, recorded from rung L0 onward via `Solver::profile()`. Each rung also pre-registers a maximum dispatch count and a substep ceiling (with a rough bandwidth estimate) before its implementation starts, so the L3 wall-clock gate cannot be discovered unreachable only after the L1/L2 investment. Sparsity uses over-dispatch + early-out, never `dispatch_workgroups_indirect`.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:63:- KTD-3 — **Darcy drag is a velocity body force folded semi-implicitly into the grid update — never a divergence penalty.** Form: drag rate β(φ) from `src/models/permeability.rs` (Kozeny–Carman), applied per node between the two velocity fields via the analytic exponential (Laibe–Price) update `Δv⁺ = Δv·exp(−β·dt)` — exact for frozen-coefficient linear drag at any stiffness (β refreshes per substep; when β varies in-step through φ, slip, or Re the update is formally first-order, so the honest claim is robust stability at coffee's worst-case density ratio ρ_f/ρ_s ≈ 0.83, not unconditional exactness), momentum-conserving by construction (mass-weighted pair update). Main's divergence-penalty formulation is recorded as a named anti-pattern: it blocks interface entry flux and is the root cause of the stuck water layer. This per-node analytic fold is the same structure production Euler-Euler codes use for stiff interphase drag (Fluent PC-SIMPLE's Partial Elimination Algorithm; MFiX-Exa reports drag iteration counts dropping from 50+ to 3–5 with it) — a 2×2 closed-form inversion, zero global communication. Two production refinements are adopted: (1) **drag-law blending** — Kozeny-Carman/Ergun is the packed-bed law and over-predicts drag in dilute regions (pour entry, splashed grains); blend toward a dilute-suspension law (Wen-Yu form) below φ_s ≈ 0.2 with a smooth transition (the Gidaspow/Huilin-Gidaspow pattern every TFM code ships); (2) if residual added-mass jitter appears despite the exponential fold, the standard stabilizer is a **virtual-mass term** (C_vm ≈ 0.5) added symmetrically to both phases — a named lever, default off.
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:126:- **Requirements:** R6, R8, R10
docs/plans/2026-06-09-001-feat-unified-twofield-solver-plan.md:140:- **Approach:** Quadratic B-spline weights (KEEP.md §3 validated math), fixed-point `atomic<i32>` P2G scatter with documented FP_SCALE/velocity-cap headroom, per-particle affine C matrix carried and updated in G2P. Gravity on the grid, plus grid-node solid-boundary treatment against the scene SDF solids (separating/no-penetration condition on grid velocities, mirroring `src/utils/sdf.rs` conventions with a "mirrors utils::sdf" WGSL comment) — boundary handling is scoped work here, not an afterthought. No pressure yet — this unit's water is a momentum-correct splashing dust.
